1
00:00:31,771 --> 00:00:38,953
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what do you think about the thought that having pets increases one's attachments in the world?

2
00:00:43,065 --> 00:00:49,148
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, the brief answer is, things in the world are not your attachment.

3
00:00:50,288 --> 00:00:53,790
[SPEAKER_03]: It's your attitude towards the things in the world.

4
00:00:53,810 --> 00:01:01,673
[SPEAKER_03]: In the same sense, the Buddha said, beautiful things in the world are not man's sensualities, desire and lust towards those things.

5
00:01:01,693 --> 00:01:02,894
[SPEAKER_03]: The sensuality is there.

6
00:01:04,753 --> 00:01:10,876
[SPEAKER_03]: So, people blaming things for their attachment, means people are not seeing where their attachment is.

7
00:01:11,296 --> 00:01:14,158
[SPEAKER_03]: So, as long as you blame things for your attachment, you're gonna be attached.

8
00:01:15,530 --> 00:01:22,194
[SPEAKER_03]: Because the most natural response is, when people think, I must give up the attachment, is to throw away all my possessions and get rid of everything.

9
00:01:22,815 --> 00:01:35,503
[SPEAKER_03]: But that's not freeing you from attachment, that's just obscuring your attitude that you had towards those things, which means, when something else comes your way, you're going to be equally attached to it, because you haven't undermined that, eh... a mental stance of appropriation.

10
00:01:39,350 --> 00:01:55,223
[SPEAKER_03]: So, in terms of pets or family members, parents, like, your attachment is your emotional dependence upon those things being present in the world, not those things in the world.

11
00:01:56,003 --> 00:02:04,130
[SPEAKER_03]: Because if truly you would need to get rid of the stuff to be free from attachment, means you need to go and destroy the whole world to be free from attachment to the world.

12
00:02:04,370 --> 00:02:05,571
[SPEAKER_03]: But that's not how it works.

13
00:02:14,678 --> 00:02:21,064
[SPEAKER_01]: There are some things, some relationships not worthy of having.

14
00:02:21,104 --> 00:02:22,326
[SPEAKER_03]: Of course, yeah.

15
00:02:22,426 --> 00:02:23,747
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, sensuality in general.

16
00:02:25,088 --> 00:02:32,075
[SPEAKER_03]: So, like, if you want to have pets, in this particular example, well, before you do, ask yourself why.

17
00:02:33,396 --> 00:02:36,179
[SPEAKER_03]: Are you having pets because you genuinely want to help animals?

18
00:02:36,822 --> 00:02:43,005
[SPEAKER_03]: We are having pets because you're feeling lonely, or bored, or you need... you need excitement, or some novelty, or just a cute little pet.

19
00:02:43,425 --> 00:02:44,025
[SPEAKER_03]: Which is...

20
00:02:44,466 --> 00:02:45,726
[SPEAKER_03]: Most people don't think this through.

21
00:02:45,746 --> 00:02:49,168
[SPEAKER_03]: That's why, like, the shelters are full of abandoned animals in the West.

22
00:02:50,188 --> 00:03:04,515
[SPEAKER_03]: Because people would take them in a... in a spur of a moment, and then afterwards, basically, when that kind of novelty runs out, you realize it's... it's... it's too demanding, too much care, too... it takes too much time, too much effort, too much responsibility.

23
00:03:05,389 --> 00:03:11,660
[SPEAKER_03]: Then, you just give them, well, the places that sort of provide it, but really what you do is just make it someone else's problem.

24
00:03:13,183 --> 00:03:15,346
[SPEAKER_03]: And that all could have been prevented if you were...

25
00:03:16,921 --> 00:03:20,503
[SPEAKER_03]: A bit more self-reflexive before you commit it.

26
00:03:21,724 --> 00:03:26,106
[SPEAKER_03]: Obviously, you have, like, things that... like your parents or your family, for example.

27
00:03:26,827 --> 00:03:27,527
[SPEAKER_03]: You have no choice.

28
00:03:27,567 --> 00:03:30,609
[SPEAKER_03]: You can't, you know, can't give them away.

29
00:03:31,229 --> 00:03:36,432
[SPEAKER_03]: And... so... so that's why you... sometimes you're bound with things that kind of create attachment for you.

30
00:03:36,752 --> 00:03:39,594
[SPEAKER_03]: But it doesn't mean that you're not responsible for the attachment part.

31
00:03:40,995 --> 00:03:46,898
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, you're not responsible for being paired with these people, or those, this, your family, or, or, or, or, or so on.

32
00:03:47,219 --> 00:03:49,700
[SPEAKER_03]: But you are responsible if you're emotionally attached upon it.

33
00:03:51,041 --> 00:03:59,185
[SPEAKER_03]: But if you think that you can free yourself from attachment just by dismissing these people, or these things in the world, you're so... you have, like, seriously mistaken, basically.

34
00:04:00,206 --> 00:04:02,607
[SPEAKER_03]: The attachment, giving it up, it's much subtler than that.

35
00:04:04,048 --> 00:04:06,930
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if somebody has a pet,

36
00:04:08,440 --> 00:04:11,941
[SPEAKER_01]: And they see that they are quite emotionally attached to it.

37
00:04:12,781 --> 00:04:15,702
[SPEAKER_01]: What should they do if they want to free themselves from it?

38
00:04:15,722 --> 00:04:23,505
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, basically, they should contemplate the death of that pet as much as they can.

39
00:04:24,637 --> 00:04:30,601
[SPEAKER_03]: Without abandoning it, without stopping, like, withdrawing care for it and helping it.

40
00:04:31,441 --> 00:04:44,310
[SPEAKER_03]: But you just have to keep reminding yourself to the point of view where you become sort of... when you go emotionally above it, above the thought that this thing, that this pet, this friend or whatever, whatever attitude you have towards it, it's gonna go.

41
00:04:44,530 --> 00:04:44,970
[SPEAKER_03]: You will go.

42
00:04:44,990 --> 00:04:46,131
[SPEAKER_03]: You might die even before it.

43
00:04:48,632 --> 00:04:49,293
[SPEAKER_03]: And it will...

44
00:04:51,232 --> 00:04:52,313
[SPEAKER_03]: It's unpleasant.

45
00:04:52,333 --> 00:04:54,054
[SPEAKER_03]: That's where people are kind of either-or.

46
00:04:54,894 --> 00:05:01,838
[SPEAKER_03]: Either I'll be fully invested emotionally or I'll completely dismiss it and throw it away and not have anything to do with it because I'm afraid of my attachment.

47
00:05:02,578 --> 00:05:08,482
[SPEAKER_03]: The fact that you're afraid you might get attached means you're not free from possibility of attachment, means you're not free from nature of attachment.

48
00:05:10,363 --> 00:05:11,763
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's the problem.

49
00:05:11,823 --> 00:05:12,704
[SPEAKER_03]: That's not the problem.

50
00:05:17,482 --> 00:05:19,403
[SPEAKER_03]: And, like, you might... you can give away the pet.

51
00:05:19,423 --> 00:05:21,705
[SPEAKER_03]: There'll be other things you're then attached... attached to.

52
00:05:22,966 --> 00:05:27,368
[SPEAKER_03]: So, really, you want to recognize the problem and then see, okay, there are certain things that are not necessary.

53
00:05:27,389 --> 00:05:29,550
[SPEAKER_03]: A certain degree of involvement is not necessary.

54
00:05:30,090 --> 00:05:32,292
[SPEAKER_03]: And you've been doing it purely out of attachment.

55
00:05:33,119 --> 00:05:38,404
[SPEAKER_03]: Now, when you became aware of the attachment, you will naturally withdraw yourself to a certain degree.

56
00:05:38,844 --> 00:05:43,709
[SPEAKER_03]: But there is no blanket rule how much you must be withdrawn from the world and from your engagements.

57
00:05:44,329 --> 00:05:47,012
[SPEAKER_03]: Different people measure these things differently.

58
00:05:47,992 --> 00:05:49,754
[SPEAKER_03]: The engagement means different things for them.

59
00:05:49,834 --> 00:05:51,796
[SPEAKER_03]: Or some people can take more of it, some people can't.

60
00:05:52,927 --> 00:05:59,093
[SPEAKER_03]: So, that should not be the criteria, like how many pets you have, for example, or how many charities you support.

61
00:05:59,693 --> 00:06:03,737
[SPEAKER_03]: No, criteria should be how much of the attachment is there in regard to it.

62
00:06:12,275 --> 00:06:19,297
[SPEAKER_01]: And in regard to animals still, do you think it's best to leave them alone?

63
00:06:19,317 --> 00:06:20,858
[SPEAKER_01]: Not interfere as a human being?

64
00:06:21,178 --> 00:06:22,398
[SPEAKER_03]: I don't think there's a universal law.

65
00:06:22,418 --> 00:06:24,079
[SPEAKER_03]: You can do whatever you like, basically.

66
00:06:24,219 --> 00:06:26,220
[SPEAKER_03]: It doesn't mean you won't pay the price for it.

67
00:06:27,560 --> 00:06:30,521
[SPEAKER_03]: If, you know, if you're in a position to help, why wouldn't you?

68
00:06:31,908 --> 00:06:47,599
[SPEAKER_03]: I mean, even the Buddha himself said, giving even to an animal, or not even to a specific animal, just throwing away food and saying, may those animals and creatures that feed on this food, feed on this food that I've thrown away, that will bring you merit and sort of like a good reward for your actions.

69
00:06:48,239 --> 00:06:54,483
[SPEAKER_03]: Let alone, let alone helping, helping, helping humans or helping greater number of animals and so on.

70
00:06:55,344 --> 00:06:56,545
[SPEAKER_03]: But, yeah, you don't have to do it.

71
00:06:56,625 --> 00:06:57,505
[SPEAKER_03]: You don't have to do any of it.

72
00:06:58,246 --> 00:06:59,187
[SPEAKER_03]: So, like, you must help.

73
00:06:59,207 --> 00:06:59,707
[SPEAKER_03]: No, you don't.

74
00:07:01,727 --> 00:07:10,511
[SPEAKER_01]: But aren't they... some people think that animals are happier when they're not around human beings.

75
00:07:10,551 --> 00:07:11,532
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, they might think that.

76
00:07:11,692 --> 00:07:13,873
[SPEAKER_03]: And what they should do is ask themselves, why do they think that?

77
00:07:14,653 --> 00:07:21,977
[SPEAKER_03]: And you're probably going to find out that they think it because they want to find a justification for them not helping animals in the first place.

78
00:07:23,037 --> 00:07:27,640
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, when they are in a position to do... when they wouldn't even necessarily be attached to it, even to help them.

79
00:07:28,338 --> 00:07:29,178
[SPEAKER_03]: And they still don't do it.

80
00:07:29,919 --> 00:07:34,180
[SPEAKER_03]: And then they would find, oh, well, that's whatever they say, that's their karma, they deserve it, basically.

81
00:07:34,560 --> 00:07:35,421
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's just ridiculous.

82
00:07:37,962 --> 00:07:45,625
[SPEAKER_03]: Even if they do deserve it, that still has nothing to do with you choosing to not help somebody in need of help, animal or human, doesn't matter.

83
00:07:46,685 --> 00:07:50,767
[SPEAKER_03]: Now, if you don't help, yeah, you don't have to feel guilty.

84
00:07:51,047 --> 00:07:54,348
[SPEAKER_03]: It's, you know, it's really up to you to do whatever you want.

85
00:07:55,541 --> 00:08:04,184
[SPEAKER_03]: But, if you're self-honest, and if you question your motive, you'll see that usually, not helping is rooted in unwholesome motives.

86
00:08:04,204 --> 00:08:06,765
[SPEAKER_03]: Either ill-will or dismissal, can't be bothered, can't... can't...

87
00:08:06,825 --> 00:08:08,505
[SPEAKER_03]: I don't care, or too lazy, or whatever.

88
00:08:10,206 --> 00:08:17,188
[SPEAKER_03]: And if you're aware of it, you wouldn't want it.

89
00:08:18,969 --> 00:08:20,049
[SPEAKER_01]: What about neutering?

90
00:08:20,109 --> 00:08:21,710
[SPEAKER_00]: Eh, eh, eh, listen, listen.

91
00:08:23,922 --> 00:08:24,162
[SPEAKER_01]: Sorry?

92
00:08:24,402 --> 00:08:25,303
[SPEAKER_01]: What about neutering?

93
00:08:25,743 --> 00:08:26,763
[SPEAKER_01]: Neutering the animals?

94
00:08:26,783 --> 00:08:31,906
[SPEAKER_01]: The animals, your pets, so they can't have... they can't poke you?

95
00:08:32,426 --> 00:08:33,166
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, of course.

96
00:08:33,246 --> 00:08:38,889
[SPEAKER_03]: Now, to answer that question, obviously, it would... depends what point of view you come from.

97
00:08:39,169 --> 00:08:47,273
[SPEAKER_03]: But, obviously, as somebody who practices the dhamma, and a Buddhist person, it's probably the best thing you can do to an animal.

98
00:08:48,610 --> 00:09:03,217
[SPEAKER_03]: Because animals stay animals, as in, they remain bound to central... animal central realm, because all their thoughts, all their actions revolve around senses, around animal intentions, around cravings, sexuality, food, fighting.

99
00:09:03,317 --> 00:09:05,679
[SPEAKER_03]: It is constantly driven.

100
00:09:05,819 --> 00:09:10,861
[SPEAKER_03]: And because of that, it's so hard to break out of the animal rebirth.

101
00:09:10,881 --> 00:09:11,782
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's what the Buddha said.

102
00:09:11,822 --> 00:09:17,585
[SPEAKER_03]: It takes like a blind... similar of a blind turtle coming up to the top of the sea, top of the ocean, once every 500 years.

103
00:09:19,669 --> 00:09:22,230
[SPEAKER_03]: And there is a yoke, a piece of wood with a hole in it.

104
00:09:22,910 --> 00:09:27,811
[SPEAKER_03]: It's being sort of thrown and floating left and right, thrown by the winds and the waves.

105
00:09:27,891 --> 00:09:37,333
[SPEAKER_03]: And this blind turtle that comes from the bottom once 500 years, has a greater chance of sticking its head through that hole in that little piece of wood.

106
00:09:37,353 --> 00:09:38,414
[SPEAKER_03]: It's being thrown constantly.

107
00:09:38,834 --> 00:09:43,275
[SPEAKER_03]: Then, a being that has fallen into an animal realm, has a chance of coming back to human realm.

108
00:09:44,992 --> 00:09:45,172
[SPEAKER_03]: Why?

109
00:09:45,232 --> 00:09:46,213
[SPEAKER_03]: Because you look at them.

110
00:09:46,273 --> 00:09:49,074
[SPEAKER_03]: Whatever they do, it's bound with the animal realm entirely.

111
00:09:49,635 --> 00:09:58,040
[SPEAKER_03]: So, they cannot have things, sort of, eh... like a... of a loftier nature that would then result in a... in a higher rebirth.

112
00:10:00,361 --> 00:10:04,684
[SPEAKER_03]: So, why neutering is good is because animals can't restrain themselves.

113
00:10:04,784 --> 00:10:09,947
[SPEAKER_03]: The only way they can restrain themselves if... if... is if that's... that restraint is placed externally.

114
00:10:10,367 --> 00:10:11,988
[SPEAKER_03]: Mind you, that's true for most of the humans.

115
00:10:14,166 --> 00:10:15,427
[SPEAKER_03]: But, we are speaking about animals.

116
00:10:15,448 --> 00:10:22,776
[SPEAKER_03]: So, basically, the lucky animal is the one that has proper restraint placed upon it, externally, as in, by a human.

117
00:10:22,796 --> 00:10:23,697
[SPEAKER_01]: Not like violence.

118
00:10:23,997 --> 00:10:24,858
[SPEAKER_03]: No violence, no.

119
00:10:25,139 --> 00:10:29,564
[SPEAKER_03]: So, like, neutering them, as we've done with these dogs, you saw the massive difference.

120
00:10:29,584 --> 00:10:31,666
[SPEAKER_03]: It's like, they don't think about sexuality anymore.

121
00:10:32,487 --> 00:10:45,076
[SPEAKER_03]: And you do remember, when they did think about it, well, like a madness would come over their mind, which means all the actions they'll be committing on account of it are very, very unwholesome, and they have no... they don't have enough self-reflection to see that.

122
00:10:46,096 --> 00:10:51,080
[SPEAKER_03]: So, in a way, they're victims, but they're victims of their own actions, and that's why it keeps perpetuating itself.

123
00:10:52,057 --> 00:11:01,581
[SPEAKER_03]: So, the... a lucky dog, for example, or a cat, or whatever, the one that you neutered, means you took that very powerful, unwholesome dimension out of their existence, basically.

124
00:11:02,101 --> 00:11:06,062
[SPEAKER_03]: Sexuality, craving, fighting, I mean, raping, it's horrible.

125
00:11:06,502 --> 00:11:09,544
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, when they get into their mate in the heat, it's just violent.

126
00:11:09,564 --> 00:11:14,245
[SPEAKER_03]: So, when you look on the roads and streets, and they lose perspective as well, completely.

127
00:11:14,285 --> 00:11:18,187
[SPEAKER_03]: Come back malnourished, ripped apart, because they were fighting for dominance and whatnot.

128
00:11:18,827 --> 00:11:20,669
[SPEAKER_03]: Everything, just unwholesome on top of the unwholesome.

129
00:11:21,089 --> 00:11:23,591
[SPEAKER_03]: And now, you're new to them, and that whole dimension is gone.

130
00:11:23,891 --> 00:11:26,314
[SPEAKER_03]: They can't even think about it anymore, let alone act upon it.

131
00:11:27,194 --> 00:11:43,288
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you could see that in an animal realm, which could be considered a darker realm, as in all the actions are bound with sensuality, and ill-will, and craving, and fear, and so on, having an absence of the same amount of bad actions can actually mean a lot of positive.

132
00:11:44,088 --> 00:11:54,317
[SPEAKER_03]: In the same sense, what the Buddha said, in the darker realms, even just like a fleeting thought, a positive thought, even very fleeting in the nature, can mean a lot.

133
00:11:54,377 --> 00:11:54,577
[SPEAKER_03]: Why?

134
00:11:54,617 --> 00:11:55,478
[SPEAKER_03]: Because there isn't any.

135
00:11:56,279 --> 00:12:03,245
[SPEAKER_03]: So, it will really stand out and it could then bring those beings back up to higher realms, where they can become aware of their self-reflection.

136
00:12:04,653 --> 00:12:21,073
[SPEAKER_03]: And, consequently, that's why the Buddha said the people who live their life unrestrained, don't even keep the five precepts and basically just follow every single desire that they have, scratch, ditch, whenever it itches, they're no different than animals and usually that's the realm they're heading, their behavior is taking them to.

137
00:12:23,195 --> 00:12:36,788
[SPEAKER_03]: Then, you just react upon any impulse that comes your way and completely are unaware, due to your own obscuring, unaware of the responsibility that you actually inherently carry for your own choices and your own freedom.

138
00:12:37,989 --> 00:12:38,510
[SPEAKER_03]: So, that's what I mean.

139
00:12:38,590 --> 00:12:39,791
[SPEAKER_03]: People don't have to help anyone.

140
00:12:41,192 --> 00:12:46,598
[SPEAKER_03]: But you should really ask yourself, if you choose not to help, well, ask yourself why, why did you make that choice?

141
00:12:47,768 --> 00:12:48,468
[SPEAKER_03]: Why was it rooted?

142
00:12:49,029 --> 00:12:52,230
[SPEAKER_03]: Was it rooted in carelessness, laziness, hatred, whatever?

143
00:12:52,511 --> 00:12:53,391
[SPEAKER_03]: Just find it out.

144
00:12:55,192 --> 00:13:01,055
[SPEAKER_01]: So, is there any other way that you could help animals, if you wanted to, other than neutering them?

145
00:13:01,435 --> 00:13:02,956
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, you might want to do that as well.

146
00:13:03,056 --> 00:13:03,816
[SPEAKER_03]: Right.

147
00:13:03,856 --> 00:13:09,920
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, I think, obviously, just providing, like feeding them, for example, means they'll be less on a

148
00:13:10,620 --> 00:13:18,443
[SPEAKER_03]: On that verge of hunger, they will make them edgy, and in a fighting mode, and greedy, and stealing, and so on.

149
00:13:18,543 --> 00:13:19,504
[SPEAKER_03]: All these are wholesome things.

150
00:13:19,964 --> 00:13:20,944
[SPEAKER_03]: Sure, vaccinations.

151
00:13:21,364 --> 00:13:27,367
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you help them, obviously, like, if you can, by shelter, providing shelter, providing medicine, providing food.

152
00:13:27,707 --> 00:13:29,888
[SPEAKER_03]: Then, if you have the ability, train them a bit.

153
00:13:30,708 --> 00:13:31,988
[SPEAKER_03]: Just make them wait for the food.

154
00:13:33,229 --> 00:13:35,390
[SPEAKER_03]: Literally, that's something that people could benefit from.

155
00:13:36,412 --> 00:13:39,694
[SPEAKER_03]: I eat whenever I feel an urge to put something in my mouth, basically.

156
00:13:39,714 --> 00:13:40,555
[SPEAKER_03]: Not when I'm hungry.

157
00:13:41,535 --> 00:13:43,717
[SPEAKER_03]: And actually, the Buddha did say that in one of the suttas.

158
00:13:44,197 --> 00:13:51,242
[SPEAKER_03]: That in some regards, animals are actually more virtues than most of the, like, the contemporary people at the time.

159
00:13:52,110 --> 00:13:56,472
[SPEAKER_03]: So, they would mate only when there was basically a season, when a female would be in a season.

160
00:13:56,892 --> 00:14:15,138
[SPEAKER_03]: But, at the time, when they were saying that, you would have these rich people, would have, like, many wives, and just constantly having sexual intercourse, left and right, without a purpose, just with... sole purpose... sole purpose was sensuality.

161
00:14:24,988 --> 00:14:41,875
[SPEAKER_03]: The other thing that the Buddha did say was, in regards to the same sort of sutta, was the... yeah, like the animals would eat when they're hungry, the dogs, but then you had people like accruing wealth and just not sharing and just eating whenever they wanted, just hoarding it.

162
00:14:42,474 --> 00:14:43,575
[SPEAKER_03]: Being greedy, basically.

163
00:14:43,595 --> 00:14:45,597
[SPEAKER_03]: So, in that regard, animals are more restrained.

164
00:14:46,337 --> 00:15:02,852
[SPEAKER_03]: But, obviously, you know, sexuality is a big part in every being's sort of life, and certainly can be a great motivator, which always results in unwholesome proliferation of desire, which is then responsible for your rebirth and actions you committed on account of it.

165
00:15:03,683 --> 00:15:16,381
[SPEAKER_03]: So, just helping animals in that sense, you would basically prevent the whole domain of attachment for them, on account of which you'll be doing lots and lots of unwholesome actions.

166
00:15:18,023 --> 00:15:19,606
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, even for a female dog, as well.

167
00:15:21,342 --> 00:15:37,751
[SPEAKER_03]: I mean, first of all, you help her not going through that whole process of being basically gang-raped by the bunch of... by a pack of dogs, which is how... it might not happen when people are kind of carefully breeding dogs, but when you look out on the road, it's like ten males, and it's just battlefield.

168
00:15:39,012 --> 00:15:41,453
[SPEAKER_03]: For two weeks, you're just crying and abused.

169
00:15:41,493 --> 00:15:41,854
[SPEAKER_03]: Horrible.

170
00:15:42,454 --> 00:15:46,597
[SPEAKER_03]: And then, on top of it, puppies, children, are the greatest attachment.

171
00:15:46,757 --> 00:15:48,098
[SPEAKER_03]: And it's no different for animals.

172
00:15:48,438 --> 00:15:51,240
[SPEAKER_03]: So, people think, oh, you're depriving these animals of happiness.

173
00:15:51,620 --> 00:15:58,565
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, well, that happiness is just like one percent, on top of the ninety-nine percent of pain and exposure to pain.

174
00:16:00,699 --> 00:16:05,760
[SPEAKER_03]: Not just that she can barely fend for herself and provide for herself, like a stray dog, for example.

175
00:16:06,221 --> 00:16:07,461
[SPEAKER_03]: Now, she seeks puppies to feed.

176
00:16:07,541 --> 00:16:09,702
[SPEAKER_03]: And you see that, when you see the stray dog with puppies.

177
00:16:10,802 --> 00:16:17,284
[SPEAKER_03]: When she starts breastfeeding, it just turns into this completely emaciated sort of skeleton, like within a week.

178
00:16:18,324 --> 00:16:19,284
[SPEAKER_03]: Because they don't get enough.

179
00:16:19,324 --> 00:16:20,945
[SPEAKER_03]: And then, you think that's pleasant.

180
00:16:22,665 --> 00:16:24,306
[SPEAKER_03]: It's pleasant for the dog and for the puppies.

181
00:16:24,386 --> 00:16:24,626
[SPEAKER_03]: It isn't.

182
00:16:24,646 --> 00:16:25,626
[SPEAKER_03]: It's just completely the opposite.

183
00:16:25,646 --> 00:16:26,346
[SPEAKER_03]: It's a torture.

184
00:16:26,566 --> 00:16:27,987
[SPEAKER_03]: And most of the puppies will end up dying.

185
00:16:29,756 --> 00:16:30,937
[SPEAKER_03]: And probably the mother as well.

186
00:16:31,017 --> 00:16:36,358
[SPEAKER_03]: If she doesn't die, clearly, it's going to take her like half a year to recover, to have the fur back and health back.

187
00:16:36,758 --> 00:16:38,399
[SPEAKER_03]: By which time, she's going to get pregnant again.

188
00:16:39,519 --> 00:16:40,199
[SPEAKER_03]: And then again.

189
00:16:40,339 --> 00:16:49,402
[SPEAKER_03]: And it's just a constant sort of being subjected to just a constant pressure and striving and greed and fear and fighting.

190
00:16:49,622 --> 00:16:50,362
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

191
00:16:51,183 --> 00:16:53,884
[SPEAKER_03]: And you remove that, then it's just a happy dog.

192
00:16:57,046 --> 00:17:00,829
[SPEAKER_01]: And what if your dogs get very sick?

193
00:17:04,112 --> 00:17:06,354
[SPEAKER_01]: And one of them are in a lot of pain.

194
00:17:08,415 --> 00:17:09,096
[SPEAKER_01]: There's no cure.

195
00:17:11,898 --> 00:17:14,000
[SPEAKER_01]: Would you consider euthanasia?

196
00:17:14,020 --> 00:17:14,680
[SPEAKER_01]: No, of course not.

197
00:17:14,720 --> 00:17:15,101
[SPEAKER_03]: Actually, no.

198
00:17:17,122 --> 00:17:17,783
[SPEAKER_03]: They'll die anyway.

199
00:17:17,803 --> 00:17:18,944
[SPEAKER_03]: We're all gonna die anyway.

200
00:17:21,697 --> 00:17:26,738
[SPEAKER_03]: If a dog were to have a choice to do it itself, yeah, I wouldn't interfere with it either.

201
00:17:27,338 --> 00:17:27,798
[SPEAKER_03]: But I don't.

202
00:17:28,639 --> 00:17:31,259
[SPEAKER_03]: Which means you'll be killing it.

203
00:17:31,299 --> 00:17:32,699
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if it was a lot of pain?

204
00:17:32,939 --> 00:17:33,480
[SPEAKER_03]: It doesn't matter.

205
00:17:34,740 --> 00:17:45,042
[SPEAKER_03]: Because the pains that you can perceive in someone else's dying might not be anything in comparison to the pains of a being actually dying.

206
00:17:47,803 --> 00:17:51,146
[SPEAKER_03]: In other words, like, it might look painful, but it's still alive.

207
00:17:51,286 --> 00:17:53,908
[SPEAKER_03]: But when it actually starts dying, that's the worst type of pain.

208
00:17:53,968 --> 00:17:55,329
[SPEAKER_03]: And that is according to the Buddha.

209
00:17:55,930 --> 00:17:58,652
[SPEAKER_03]: But usually people judge this situation based on how they feel.

210
00:17:58,792 --> 00:18:01,114
[SPEAKER_03]: Oh, I can't bear to watch this, so I'll kill it.

211
00:18:02,275 --> 00:18:03,036
[SPEAKER_03]: Compassionately.

212
00:18:03,356 --> 00:18:04,777
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll put it out of my misery.

213
00:18:05,017 --> 00:18:05,738
[SPEAKER_03]: Pretty much, yeah.

214
00:18:05,798 --> 00:18:07,219
[SPEAKER_03]: I'll put it out of my misery, yeah.

215
00:18:07,399 --> 00:18:08,580
[SPEAKER_03]: That's that's how you should read.

216
00:18:11,563 --> 00:18:14,805
[SPEAKER_03]: I mean, again, you can do whatever you want, but don't deceive yourself.

217
00:18:14,945 --> 00:18:15,706
[SPEAKER_03]: You are killing it.

218
00:18:18,208 --> 00:18:19,969
[SPEAKER_01]: But what about, like, dog shelters?

219
00:18:19,989 --> 00:18:21,529
[SPEAKER_01]: They get all these stray dogs.

220
00:18:21,669 --> 00:18:22,790
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, they're killing it.

221
00:18:22,830 --> 00:18:23,590
[SPEAKER_01]: 20,000 dogs.

222
00:18:25,230 --> 00:18:25,791
[SPEAKER_03]: They're killing it.

223
00:18:27,211 --> 00:18:38,535
[SPEAKER_03]: But, I mean, obviously, they're responsible for the act of gassing them, and whatever means they use, but whoever gives their dog to a shelter, is also responsible for killing them.

224
00:18:40,095 --> 00:18:41,656
[SPEAKER_03]: If they end up being killed.

225
00:18:41,676 --> 00:18:44,797
[SPEAKER_03]: Because they wouldn't have ended up being killed if you didn't put them in a shelter that kills dogs.

226
00:18:47,517 --> 00:18:48,118
[SPEAKER_03]: So, that's what I mean.

227
00:18:48,398 --> 00:18:57,286
[SPEAKER_03]: Think twice before you actually choose to act and commit and do things in any area of life.

228
00:18:59,688 --> 00:19:03,632
[SPEAKER_03]: But people's judgment, basically, it's usually emotional, which is not really a judgment.

229
00:19:04,212 --> 00:19:05,714
[SPEAKER_03]: It's more a reaction.

230
00:19:06,435 --> 00:19:06,995
[SPEAKER_03]: I want this.

231
00:19:07,536 --> 00:19:07,916
[SPEAKER_03]: I have it.

232
00:19:08,036 --> 00:19:11,980
[SPEAKER_03]: And then, I'm only going to look at the reasons for having it.

233
00:19:12,988 --> 00:19:19,271
[SPEAKER_03]: I'm not gonna stop and think about and maybe be patient and see if I change my mind or if I really thought this through.

234
00:19:20,732 --> 00:19:23,093
[SPEAKER_03]: And it's not just for the pets, in general, in general.

235
00:19:23,933 --> 00:19:24,994
[SPEAKER_03]: That's the attitude, really.

236
00:19:55,041 --> 00:20:07,554
[SPEAKER_01]: Another question that usually comes up for a lot of people is, how can monastics be withdrawn from sensual pleasures if they are using the internet?

237
00:20:07,814 --> 00:20:14,221
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, it's... the internet for... it's a very sensual thing.

238
00:20:14,921 --> 00:20:15,402
[SPEAKER_01]: Right.

239
00:20:16,143 --> 00:20:16,463
[SPEAKER_01]: So...

240
00:20:18,487 --> 00:20:22,871
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, you use it for that particular reason.

241
00:20:23,632 --> 00:20:24,993
[SPEAKER_03]: Basically, Internet is a tool.

242
00:20:26,435 --> 00:20:34,302
[SPEAKER_03]: So, if you use tool to gain sensuality, yeah, but you don't blame the tool, you blame your own sensual intentions and your own sensual actions.

243
00:20:35,942 --> 00:20:46,888
[SPEAKER_03]: You cannot engage in sensuality without having, sort of, without perceived it as a central desire, as a central outcome that you want to pursue.

244
00:20:47,388 --> 00:20:49,229
[SPEAKER_03]: Which means sensuality is always intentional.

245
00:20:49,289 --> 00:20:53,351
[SPEAKER_03]: You can't accidentally stumble, like, engage in sensuality.

246
00:20:55,472 --> 00:20:58,994
[SPEAKER_03]: So, the question here, it's not like whether you should use the internet or not.

247
00:20:59,074 --> 00:21:01,015
[SPEAKER_03]: The question is, are you restrained or not?

248
00:21:03,918 --> 00:21:09,542
[SPEAKER_03]: If you're not restrained, then, yeah, you should foresee these circumstances, they might tempt you further, and then you avoid it.

249
00:21:09,582 --> 00:21:17,207
[SPEAKER_03]: But even then, you don't place responsibility on avoidance of these things, because the real problem is you not having uprooted sensuality.

250
00:21:17,807 --> 00:21:26,413
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you might avoid Internet all you want, but you can still spend your time with sensual thoughts, sensual cravings, living on top of a mountain, and be equally bound by it.

251
00:21:26,891 --> 00:21:28,492
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, one day, you have to use the internet again.

252
00:21:28,572 --> 00:21:37,477
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, sooner or later, something will happen, where you're going to be exposed to things that used to cause you, basically, sensual desire, and you're going to have it again, because you haven't done anything about it.

253
00:21:39,668 --> 00:21:41,469
[SPEAKER_03]: You can't spend your whole life in hiding.

254
00:21:42,270 --> 00:21:58,800
[SPEAKER_03]: And there was that very nice sutta, the simile of the bait, the deer herd, that the Buddha talks about, where like most people would... the hunter places the food that has a bait in it, and that's basically like a description, a simile for sensuality.

255
00:21:59,101 --> 00:22:04,064
[SPEAKER_03]: It baits you to become sort of engaged with it and attached, just because of that promised pleasure.

256
00:22:05,372 --> 00:22:10,274
[SPEAKER_03]: So, the most people would be sort of careless, and they would just be eating it together with a bait.

257
00:22:10,294 --> 00:22:11,654
[SPEAKER_03]: They wouldn't even see it as a problem.

258
00:22:11,674 --> 00:22:12,675
[SPEAKER_03]: They wouldn't even see the bait.

259
00:22:13,775 --> 00:22:25,479
[SPEAKER_03]: Then, a group of people would recognize that, and then say, well, okay, we need to restrain ourselves, because that's basically... but they can't draw a line between a food and the bait, so then they dismiss the entire domain of food.

260
00:22:25,559 --> 00:22:30,301
[SPEAKER_03]: And they, as Buddha said, they're like a deer herd that runs on top of a mountain, and then they live lives there.

261
00:22:31,306 --> 00:22:42,922
[SPEAKER_03]: But sooner or later, the food will... whatever they've been living on in the mountain will dry out, and then they will come back to the same environment that they despised, and they would eat carelessly, they wouldn't see the bait, and they would remain the same as the first herd.

262
00:22:44,300 --> 00:22:48,121
[SPEAKER_03]: And then the third one would be basically the one who's seen the first, seen the second.

263
00:22:48,581 --> 00:22:51,422
[SPEAKER_03]: And then realize, actually, we must not shun the food.

264
00:22:52,042 --> 00:22:56,844
[SPEAKER_03]: We just have to learn how to eat it carefully, without engaging with the bait, without being hooked.

265
00:22:57,684 --> 00:22:58,424
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's the whole point.

266
00:22:59,044 --> 00:23:01,545
[SPEAKER_03]: But that refers to basically avoiding sensuality.

267
00:23:01,865 --> 00:23:05,026
[SPEAKER_03]: Not like flirting with sensuality and carefully, so you don't get... No.

268
00:23:05,526 --> 00:23:07,907
[SPEAKER_03]: Flirting with sensuality means you're basically already hooked.

269
00:23:09,170 --> 00:23:15,633
[SPEAKER_03]: But learning how to eat carefully or, in other words, learning how to be restrained and seeing where your responsibility is.

270
00:23:18,114 --> 00:23:18,915
[SPEAKER_03]: Because that's what I mean.

271
00:23:18,935 --> 00:23:24,998
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, people would blame the food, blame the internet, blame this, blame the city, blame the environment, but that's not the problem.

272
00:23:25,398 --> 00:23:28,659
[SPEAKER_03]: The problem is your attitude of sensuality in regard to it.

273
00:23:32,421 --> 00:23:36,443
[SPEAKER_03]: So, if you recognize that that's on you, you exercise restraint to the right degree.

274
00:23:38,080 --> 00:23:40,062
[SPEAKER_03]: And then you could see genuinely, oh, this is a tool.

275
00:23:40,483 --> 00:23:42,205
[SPEAKER_03]: This can be used wholesomely.

276
00:23:42,666 --> 00:23:44,608
[SPEAKER_03]: Obviously, this cannot be used wholesomely.

277
00:23:46,110 --> 00:23:50,976
[SPEAKER_03]: Things like sexual intercourse, they're fully already perverted by the desire, which means you can't do it rightly.

278
00:23:51,296 --> 00:23:52,698
[SPEAKER_03]: You have to abandon it altogether.

279
00:24:04,976 --> 00:24:07,357
[SPEAKER_03]: So, if a person does have problem with the restraint, sure.

280
00:24:07,877 --> 00:24:10,978
[SPEAKER_03]: Minimize your exposure to things that are challenging you.

281
00:24:11,158 --> 00:24:15,860
[SPEAKER_03]: But, don't project your responsibility onto those things.

282
00:24:15,880 --> 00:24:16,280
[SPEAKER_01]: It's on you.

283
00:24:18,101 --> 00:24:18,821
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to see it.

284
00:24:19,061 --> 00:24:19,662
[SPEAKER_01]: Your weakness.

285
00:24:19,822 --> 00:24:20,382
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, exactly.

286
00:24:20,402 --> 00:24:23,023
[SPEAKER_03]: You have to see it and then you have to see what the problem is.

287
00:24:23,203 --> 00:24:24,523
[SPEAKER_03]: The problem is not the Internet.

288
00:24:24,543 --> 00:24:26,244
[SPEAKER_03]: The problem is not the central objects.

289
00:24:26,884 --> 00:24:29,025
[SPEAKER_03]: The problem is your sensuality in regard to it.

290
00:24:30,946 --> 00:24:34,027
[SPEAKER_03]: And you're not going to approve that just by dismissing the central objects.

291
00:24:35,020 --> 00:24:38,923
[SPEAKER_03]: Or the object that could potentially result in sensual outcome.

292
00:24:40,945 --> 00:24:50,232
[SPEAKER_03]: And if you start clarifying that, then, yeah, then you are developing enough space, basically, to see and to move about without taking things up in sensual manner.

293
00:25:01,603 --> 00:25:05,627
[SPEAKER_01]: You become quite strong, because you can withstand the possibilities.

294
00:25:06,588 --> 00:25:09,130
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, not just withstand, you actually uproot it.

295
00:25:10,472 --> 00:25:14,476
[SPEAKER_03]: The possibility of you sensually engaging in sensual possibilities.

296
00:25:15,496 --> 00:25:17,318
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's when you know you're beyond sensuality.

297
00:25:19,040 --> 00:25:20,641
[SPEAKER_03]: That's like no amount of sensual

298
00:25:21,759 --> 00:25:31,524
[SPEAKER_03]: Objects would become a temptation anymore, because the mind has fully seen and discerned the danger of sensuality and the inherent, basically, repulsiveness of it.

299
00:25:32,564 --> 00:25:35,545
[SPEAKER_03]: Being a victim to your own desire, you fully understood that.

300
00:25:36,346 --> 00:25:38,807
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's... and by doing so, you have abandoned it.

301
00:25:40,468 --> 00:25:42,669
[SPEAKER_03]: So, then, you can't actually engage with it.

302
00:25:42,749 --> 00:25:44,069
[SPEAKER_03]: And then, like, it's inconceivable.

303
00:25:44,869 --> 00:25:50,191
[SPEAKER_03]: The Buddha would say it's like as if a man would sort of eat some delicious food and then he would vomit it out.

304
00:25:51,211 --> 00:25:54,232
[SPEAKER_03]: And, like, would he have a desire to eat that vomit again?

305
00:25:54,272 --> 00:25:54,852
[SPEAKER_03]: He just wouldn't.

306
00:25:55,372 --> 00:25:56,232
[SPEAKER_03]: It's inconceivable.

307
00:25:56,673 --> 00:25:58,193
[SPEAKER_03]: You'd be repelled by the thought of it.

308
00:26:14,441 --> 00:26:29,929
[SPEAKER_01]: As you said earlier about how to overcome attachments, like to your dogs or to your pets, I mean, that to contemplate the possibility, the inevitable possibility of their death, to free you from attachment.

309
00:26:29,949 --> 00:26:38,454
[SPEAKER_03]: When I say contemplate, I mean allow yourself to emotionally feel the significance of those things, not like explain to yourself

310
00:26:39,094 --> 00:26:45,077
[SPEAKER_03]: Just basically to appease your own emotional disturbance, at the thought of your favorite pet dying or something.

311
00:26:46,538 --> 00:26:48,599
[SPEAKER_03]: No, don't go, they all die and then you start explaining.

312
00:26:48,639 --> 00:26:48,759
[SPEAKER_03]: No.

313
00:26:48,999 --> 00:26:51,260
[SPEAKER_03]: You actually want to take it on that emotional level.

314
00:26:52,701 --> 00:26:56,442
[SPEAKER_03]: Basically, to not be afraid to feel the death of the loved ones.

315
00:26:59,264 --> 00:27:01,605
[SPEAKER_03]: And that will basically pressure your mind into growth.

316
00:27:02,325 --> 00:27:03,626
[SPEAKER_03]: Your mind will have to grow out of it.

317
00:27:04,666 --> 00:27:05,987
[SPEAKER_03]: Or it will have to succumb to it.

318
00:27:09,649 --> 00:27:18,637
[SPEAKER_01]: It reminds me of what you're saying this morning about being intoxicated with youth, intoxicated with health, intoxicated with life.

319
00:27:18,657 --> 00:27:19,397
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure, sure.

320
00:27:20,298 --> 00:27:20,979
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, yeah.

321
00:27:22,220 --> 00:27:36,131
[SPEAKER_03]: It's, it's... Well, take it for granted, your own life, you start taking for granted lives of other things, beings, animals or humans, which means death, even a notion of it, is going to be frightening.

322
00:27:37,570 --> 00:27:40,732
[SPEAKER_03]: To you, to your emotional equilibrium, it's going to be a challenge.

323
00:27:41,932 --> 00:27:48,316
[SPEAKER_03]: So, unless you recognize that as a problem, you can't sort of grow out, you can't develop yourself out of it.

324
00:27:48,396 --> 00:27:48,896
[SPEAKER_03]: You just can't.

325
00:27:49,757 --> 00:27:53,379
[SPEAKER_03]: Because you've got all of your efforts will be about covering it up.

326
00:27:53,399 --> 00:27:58,762
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, we all died and none of that, all of that is done just to prevent you from taking it on personally.

327
00:28:01,493 --> 00:28:06,657
[SPEAKER_03]: But if you start questioning, basically, just... well, the validity of that assumption, like, why do I...

328
00:28:07,438 --> 00:28:12,782
[SPEAKER_03]: I mean, yeah, we will all die, so why don't I imagine that I might be dead by this afternoon, or maybe even tomorrow?

329
00:28:13,222 --> 00:28:19,627
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, why would I... why would I just assume that my life is ahead of me?

330
00:28:19,687 --> 00:28:20,308
[SPEAKER_03]: Or my health?

331
00:28:20,848 --> 00:28:21,409
[SPEAKER_03]: Or my youth?

332
00:28:23,570 --> 00:28:26,673
[SPEAKER_03]: And if you start questioning those things on the level of yourself,

333
00:28:27,784 --> 00:28:34,492
[SPEAKER_03]: It means you are automatically affecting any attachment that you have depending upon those things in regard to others.

334
00:28:36,214 --> 00:28:41,621
[SPEAKER_03]: In other words, if you become fully aware of your own mortality, you cannot be attached to other person's life.

335
00:28:43,593 --> 00:28:47,416
[SPEAKER_03]: It doesn't mean you become like a blind, oblivious, robotic creature.

336
00:28:47,937 --> 00:28:54,662
[SPEAKER_03]: No, you're still full aware of the significance of life, of helping animals, or caring after people, or whatever.

337
00:28:55,062 --> 00:29:01,848
[SPEAKER_03]: But none of that causes that emotional intoxication of assumption that this will last forever.

338
00:29:04,050 --> 00:29:11,156
[SPEAKER_03]: Which means when it changes, when it goes away different to the way you expected it and hoped for, you won't be affected by it.

339
00:29:12,118 --> 00:29:16,766
[SPEAKER_03]: Your perspective will remain, basically, eh... undiminished.

340
00:29:16,866 --> 00:29:17,908
[SPEAKER_03]: It just would not change.

341
00:29:19,590 --> 00:29:21,694
[SPEAKER_03]: Because it wasn't dependent upon that anymore.

342
00:29:23,276 --> 00:29:25,821
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's the true meaning of being free from attachment and freedom.

343
00:29:26,647 --> 00:29:32,214
[SPEAKER_03]: Not like not recognizing the significance of, you know, dying loved ones or something like that.

344
00:29:32,334 --> 00:29:32,474
[SPEAKER_03]: No.

345
00:29:33,075 --> 00:29:39,503
[SPEAKER_03]: On the contrary, being fully able to let it arise to the extent it wants to arise and you won't have to do anything about it.

346
00:29:40,043 --> 00:29:43,387
[SPEAKER_03]: You don't have to jump into grief, break down into tears, nothing.

347
00:29:43,447 --> 00:29:46,251
[SPEAKER_03]: You're able to hold it and keep perspective in regard to it.

348
00:29:48,705 --> 00:29:52,887
[SPEAKER_03]: As those enlightened people in the suttas would say, like, how could it be otherwise?

349
00:29:53,167 --> 00:30:02,652
[SPEAKER_03]: When they were facing somebody dying, their good friend or mother, father, whatever, it would be just like, like, how could it be otherwise?

350
00:30:02,832 --> 00:30:05,273
[SPEAKER_03]: How could a thing that has a reason not die?

351
00:30:06,854 --> 00:30:07,735
[SPEAKER_01]: It's no surprise.

352
00:30:07,835 --> 00:30:08,615
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't surprise.

353
00:30:09,115 --> 00:30:14,498
[SPEAKER_03]: No, it's... well, it's surprising to think that it can be otherwise.

354
00:30:14,718 --> 00:30:16,499
[SPEAKER_03]: That's a surprise.

355
00:30:16,559 --> 00:30:18,040
[SPEAKER_03]: For those who cannot think that anyway.

356
00:30:38,547 --> 00:30:51,975
[SPEAKER_01]: There's very much a lot of stoicism, very much in this think about pre-meditate, think about death, think about loss of health, aging.

357
00:30:52,946 --> 00:30:55,247
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, the right thinking, basically.

358
00:30:55,427 --> 00:30:56,167
[SPEAKER_03]: The right thought.

359
00:30:56,207 --> 00:31:01,928
[SPEAKER_03]: People think right thoughts will come out spontaneously out of some sort of magical experience.

360
00:31:02,008 --> 00:31:02,168
[SPEAKER_03]: No.

361
00:31:02,228 --> 00:31:08,470
[SPEAKER_03]: Right thoughts will come up if you stop engaging, nurturing and maintaining the wrong thought.

362
00:31:09,450 --> 00:31:18,152
[SPEAKER_03]: And the wrong thought is any thought of greed, aversion, delusion, any thought that is based upon assumptions, that perpetuates these gratuitous assumptions and intoxications.

363
00:31:19,220 --> 00:31:21,002
[SPEAKER_03]: With your life, with your health...

364
00:31:21,022 --> 00:31:24,344
[SPEAKER_03]: I mean, people think, just like, when they're healthy, you think you're never gonna get sick.

365
00:31:25,325 --> 00:31:28,328
[SPEAKER_03]: And then, just the thought that you might get sick, freaks you out all the way.

366
00:31:29,149 --> 00:31:32,692
[SPEAKER_03]: It's like nothing... there's no... no... no safe zone in between.

367
00:31:33,713 --> 00:31:36,935
[SPEAKER_03]: It's like when people get a flu, they just full-on panic.

368
00:31:36,995 --> 00:31:47,024
[SPEAKER_03]: They want every medicine from the shelf, doctor to give them, just... just to get rid of this discomfort, because it's threatening to my existential assumption of... of an indefinite health.

369
00:31:48,564 --> 00:31:50,265
[SPEAKER_01]: Or a pimple on the face.

370
00:31:50,846 --> 00:31:51,987
[SPEAKER_03]: Oh, yeah, yeah, sure, sure.

371
00:31:52,047 --> 00:31:57,670
[SPEAKER_03]: A pimple on the face or a... Or a hair going the other way you don't want it to go or something.

372
00:31:57,690 --> 00:31:59,852
[SPEAKER_03]: You're just interfering with your perception of yourself.

373
00:32:01,513 --> 00:32:04,034
[SPEAKER_03]: And it's like bothering you on that existential level.

374
00:32:04,094 --> 00:32:05,355
[SPEAKER_03]: No, it's not like... it's not optional.

375
00:32:06,716 --> 00:32:08,537
[SPEAKER_03]: You can't remain indifferent towards it.

376
00:32:08,557 --> 00:32:10,379
[SPEAKER_03]: You are being emotionally oppressed.

377
00:32:11,980 --> 00:32:13,801
[SPEAKER_03]: And you're doing it yourself, basically.

378
00:32:15,630 --> 00:32:26,076
[SPEAKER_03]: By maintaining those assumptions, when they're challenged by the circumstances of the world, they go against your desires, basically.

379
00:32:26,176 --> 00:32:26,656
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

380
00:32:26,716 --> 00:32:27,917
[SPEAKER_03]: You cannot remain indifferent.

381
00:32:27,937 --> 00:32:28,657
[SPEAKER_03]: You are affected.

382
00:32:29,697 --> 00:32:39,923
[SPEAKER_03]: So, suffering is, as the Buddha described, in the most general terms, basically, not getting what one wants and being paired with things that one doesn't want.

383
00:32:41,327 --> 00:32:44,131
[SPEAKER_03]: In brief, basically, there's five aggregate ties.

384
00:32:44,171 --> 00:32:49,298
[SPEAKER_03]: In your experience as a whole, attachment to it, in any shape or form, that's why suffering is there.

385
00:32:58,310 --> 00:33:05,032
[SPEAKER_01]: Actually, I was going to ask this question, you touched on it there, is how to improve your thinking?

386
00:33:05,632 --> 00:33:09,114
[SPEAKER_01]: How to make your thinking clearer and right?

387
00:33:11,194 --> 00:33:12,875
[SPEAKER_01]: Think about the right things.

388
00:33:12,995 --> 00:33:13,415
[SPEAKER_01]: Deeply.

389
00:33:13,895 --> 00:33:15,035
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, that's the thing.

390
00:33:15,055 --> 00:33:21,337
[SPEAKER_03]: The first step is to get a hint, at least, that actually this might be a valuable thing to do.

391
00:33:21,577 --> 00:33:22,878
[SPEAKER_03]: Learn how to think properly.

392
00:33:24,987 --> 00:33:35,411
[SPEAKER_03]: Then, if you do, then you can start looking closer at it and basically see what is... what are the things that are sort of against right mindful thinking.

393
00:33:35,431 --> 00:33:36,571
[SPEAKER_03]: Essential desire is.

394
00:33:36,871 --> 00:33:42,573
[SPEAKER_03]: By giving in to essential desire, to the emotional needs, you are compromising your own reason.

395
00:33:43,333 --> 00:33:52,897
[SPEAKER_03]: Now, some people do more, some people do less, but nevertheless, that's in common to every time you give in to desire or need for emotional satisfaction,

396
00:33:53,927 --> 00:33:58,630
[SPEAKER_03]: For sensual satisfaction, to that degree, you compromise your reason, you compromise your mindfulness.

397
00:33:59,591 --> 00:34:08,136
[SPEAKER_03]: Even if you're actually contemplating and premeditating how to engage in sensuality, that's not done in that rightful manner, because it's not founded upon mindfulness and self-awareness.

398
00:34:09,437 --> 00:34:12,619
[SPEAKER_03]: You are projected towards the project of sensuality or whatever else.

399
00:34:14,507 --> 00:34:20,832
[SPEAKER_03]: So, recognizing what goes against calm, composed, mindful thinking.

400
00:34:21,333 --> 00:34:21,613
[SPEAKER_01]: Clear.

401
00:34:21,993 --> 00:34:22,273
[SPEAKER_01]: Clear.

402
00:34:22,333 --> 00:34:23,234
[SPEAKER_03]: Clarity, exactly.

403
00:34:23,374 --> 00:34:24,355
[SPEAKER_01]: Foggy.

404
00:34:24,795 --> 00:34:27,958
[SPEAKER_03]: Foggy, obtuse, opaque, whatever.

405
00:34:28,238 --> 00:34:28,999
[SPEAKER_03]: It's all against it.

406
00:34:29,039 --> 00:34:31,781
[SPEAKER_03]: So, ill-will, anger, it's against it.

407
00:34:32,542 --> 00:34:40,248
[SPEAKER_03]: Engaging in angry behavior, cultivating ill-will and hatred, you are basically obscuring your own perspective on yourself.

408
00:34:41,541 --> 00:34:42,482
[SPEAKER_03]: Same with sensuality.

409
00:34:43,143 --> 00:34:44,945
[SPEAKER_03]: Same with distraction, in general.

410
00:34:45,626 --> 00:34:47,468
[SPEAKER_03]: You just want to do something because you're bored.

411
00:34:48,369 --> 00:34:49,831
[SPEAKER_03]: Boredom is actually clarity.

412
00:34:50,532 --> 00:34:51,793
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's why people fear it.

413
00:34:52,794 --> 00:34:57,300
[SPEAKER_03]: Because it reveals things that you don't want to admit to yourself, yet they're always there.

414
00:34:58,491 --> 00:35:00,392
[SPEAKER_03]: That's why people always need things to do.

415
00:35:00,612 --> 00:35:11,499
[SPEAKER_03]: Existentially, they depend upon constant performing of action and finding meaning in random things, just to make them do stuff, because it basically keeps you floating, or so it seems.

416
00:35:12,240 --> 00:35:21,285
[SPEAKER_03]: If you run out of things to do, if you run out of even the most frivolous purpose, you basically feel like you begin to sink.

417
00:35:23,827 --> 00:35:24,967
[SPEAKER_03]: But the problem with that whole

418
00:35:27,681 --> 00:35:31,823
[SPEAKER_03]: The whole view is that sooner or later you won't be able to do things.

419
00:35:33,484 --> 00:35:37,265
[SPEAKER_03]: And what you're going to have left is that which you've been running away from your whole life.

420
00:35:37,285 --> 00:35:39,106
[SPEAKER_03]: That's why old age is very unpleasant.

421
00:35:40,246 --> 00:35:43,668
[SPEAKER_03]: The body stops listening, you lost your health, you lost your strength, you lost your youth.

422
00:35:45,761 --> 00:35:46,742
[SPEAKER_03]: The senses are failing.

423
00:35:46,762 --> 00:35:49,244
[SPEAKER_03]: You can't get distracted now through beautiful sights.

424
00:35:49,304 --> 00:35:50,745
[SPEAKER_03]: You can't even appreciate it anymore.

425
00:35:50,785 --> 00:35:53,667
[SPEAKER_03]: It just hurts to look, hurts to hear it.

426
00:35:54,187 --> 00:35:55,848
[SPEAKER_03]: It's not pleasant eating.

427
00:35:55,868 --> 00:35:57,129
[SPEAKER_03]: And what you're left is yourself.

428
00:35:58,050 --> 00:35:58,851
[SPEAKER_03]: That's what you're left with.

429
00:35:58,991 --> 00:36:01,112
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's something you didn't want to be with your whole life.

430
00:36:01,693 --> 00:36:08,097
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's why you've been using the intoxication with health, youth, and life, and sensuality in general.

431
00:36:09,038 --> 00:36:14,242
[SPEAKER_03]: Just to keep sort of surfing on top of it, without having to face what's underneath.

432
00:36:16,270 --> 00:36:25,653
[SPEAKER_01]: There's a lot of clarity, awareness, constant awareness, can be very painful.

433
00:36:25,673 --> 00:36:29,054
[SPEAKER_01]: You said boredom is very painful because of clarity.

434
00:36:29,274 --> 00:36:33,295
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, clarity is not painful, but it reveals the painful background.

435
00:36:33,355 --> 00:36:35,895
[SPEAKER_03]: And that background is painful only because you're resisting it.

436
00:36:36,876 --> 00:36:43,438
[SPEAKER_03]: Only because your habits of your life, the ones you've been engaging with, are the ones that are taking you away from it.

437
00:36:43,698 --> 00:36:44,738
[SPEAKER_03]: Namely, sensuality.

438
00:36:46,288 --> 00:36:58,752
[SPEAKER_03]: Just following your desires for granted, wanting to have whatever your desire basically directs you... directs you at, and because of that, not engaging in that behavior is deemed painful.

439
00:36:59,952 --> 00:37:03,053
[SPEAKER_03]: You create the pain of boredom.

440
00:37:04,254 --> 00:37:04,934
[SPEAKER_03]: As in boredom.

441
00:37:04,994 --> 00:37:06,934
[SPEAKER_03]: You create the boredom because you don't want it.

442
00:37:08,015 --> 00:37:08,755
[SPEAKER_03]: Boredom in itself.

443
00:37:09,515 --> 00:37:11,196
[SPEAKER_03]: It's not neutral.

444
00:37:11,236 --> 00:37:11,956
[SPEAKER_03]: Sorry, it's neutral.

445
00:37:12,316 --> 00:37:13,076
[SPEAKER_03]: It's not unpleasant.

446
00:37:13,236 --> 00:37:14,657
[SPEAKER_03]: It's neither pleasant nor unpleasant.

447
00:37:15,536 --> 00:37:16,157
[SPEAKER_03]: That's what Buddha said.

448
00:37:16,177 --> 00:37:20,901
[SPEAKER_03]: If you understand neutral feeling, it is felt as pleasant.

449
00:37:20,921 --> 00:37:31,892
[SPEAKER_03]: If you don't understand it, as in, if you're still basically affecting it with assumptions and and and and preconceived notions and habits of sensuality, then it's going to feel unpleasant.

450
00:37:31,932 --> 00:37:33,654
[SPEAKER_03]: Feeling nothing is going to feel unpleasant.

451
00:37:34,314 --> 00:37:35,836
[SPEAKER_03]: It's going to feel threatening, basically.

452
00:37:41,932 --> 00:37:46,695
[SPEAKER_01]: So, do you have any practices to clear one's thoughts?

453
00:37:47,375 --> 00:37:50,337
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, physical restraint.

454
00:37:50,417 --> 00:37:54,059
[SPEAKER_03]: Not engaging in distracted behavior, sense restraint.

455
00:37:54,199 --> 00:38:06,306
[SPEAKER_03]: Not engaging in, basically, like... Yeah, you have to stop, for example, drinking, being careless, taking drugs, because that, certainly, it's directly opposed to any clarity that you can maintain in your mind.

456
00:38:07,947 --> 00:38:08,828
[SPEAKER_03]: Then, at the same time,

457
00:38:09,705 --> 00:38:14,466
[SPEAKER_03]: You also have to understand that the real clarity comes from understanding what clarity is.

458
00:38:16,026 --> 00:38:17,506
[SPEAKER_03]: Not from what feels clear.

459
00:38:18,086 --> 00:38:19,287
[SPEAKER_03]: That's inevitable at the beginning.

460
00:38:19,367 --> 00:38:22,807
[SPEAKER_03]: We all start based on how we feel, but you want to transcend that.

461
00:38:22,847 --> 00:38:26,588
[SPEAKER_03]: You don't want to just be bound by how you feel and what you deem pleasant or unpleasant.

462
00:38:27,928 --> 00:38:35,910
[SPEAKER_03]: Because if people don't understand the clarity, then they're just going to be pursuing that which they think clarity is, which is that which they deem agreeable.

463
00:38:37,050 --> 00:38:38,090
[SPEAKER_03]: Not necessarily understood.

464
00:38:39,152 --> 00:38:44,594
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's when you have people overdoing the practice of clarity, trying to catch every single moment and things like that.

465
00:38:45,155 --> 00:38:47,476
[SPEAKER_03]: But that's a contradiction in terms.

466
00:38:47,496 --> 00:38:50,217
[SPEAKER_03]: You know, to catch a moment, you have to be outside of that moment.

467
00:38:50,337 --> 00:38:52,738
[SPEAKER_03]: But if you're outside of that moment, that moment is not in your experience.

468
00:38:53,618 --> 00:38:59,421
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you're just chasing these hollow, contradictory ideas on account of developing clarity.

469
00:39:01,281 --> 00:39:04,923
[SPEAKER_03]: So, clarity is basically absence of greed, aversion, delusion.

470
00:39:06,458 --> 00:39:12,383
[SPEAKER_03]: If those things are not present in your experience, you don't have to then get rid of whatever else is present.

471
00:39:13,784 --> 00:39:16,106
[SPEAKER_03]: Because all you care about is the unwholesome aspect.

472
00:39:17,407 --> 00:39:22,210
[SPEAKER_03]: But people usually can't see where the line is, so then they try to dismiss it all.

473
00:39:22,370 --> 00:39:24,252
[SPEAKER_03]: In the same sense, when we spoke just earlier,

474
00:39:25,578 --> 00:39:28,480
[SPEAKER_03]: Attachment is a problem, so I'll throw away everything.

475
00:39:28,500 --> 00:39:29,560
[SPEAKER_03]: Baby and the bathwater.

476
00:39:29,861 --> 00:39:31,101
[SPEAKER_03]: The whole thing.

477
00:39:31,121 --> 00:39:31,882
[SPEAKER_03]: Oh, I'm attached to this.

478
00:39:31,922 --> 00:39:32,522
[SPEAKER_03]: I must destroy it.

479
00:39:32,542 --> 00:39:33,183
[SPEAKER_03]: I must get rid of it.

480
00:39:33,223 --> 00:39:33,803
[SPEAKER_03]: I must give it up.

481
00:39:34,263 --> 00:39:36,485
[SPEAKER_03]: That's how I free myself from my attachment, what you want.

482
00:39:37,525 --> 00:39:43,329
[SPEAKER_03]: And the Buddha realized that when he came to the point of even trying to give up his own life, because thinking that's the way out, but then he realized it wasn't.

483
00:39:43,909 --> 00:39:46,151
[SPEAKER_03]: It's only gonna result in me dying, unenlightened.

484
00:39:47,311 --> 00:39:49,353
[SPEAKER_03]: When he tried to stop his own breathing, basically.

485
00:39:49,393 --> 00:39:50,293
[SPEAKER_03]: He said, well, I'm breathing.

486
00:39:50,834 --> 00:39:52,134
[SPEAKER_03]: I'm helping this life that's a

487
00:39:55,399 --> 00:39:56,360
[SPEAKER_03]: Unfortunately, he didn't.

488
00:39:56,420 --> 00:40:00,702
[SPEAKER_03]: He saw through it and then he realized that actually, no, it's only resulting in me being dead.

489
00:40:00,982 --> 00:40:01,603
[SPEAKER_03]: Nothing like that.

490
00:40:05,385 --> 00:40:18,012
[SPEAKER_03]: So, abandon things that are unnecessary, with basically a full proliferation of carelessness in regard to sensuality, ill-will, restraint and moderation.

491
00:40:19,833 --> 00:40:22,475
[SPEAKER_03]: And then, try to understand that clarity.

492
00:40:23,395 --> 00:40:24,336
[SPEAKER_03]: Don't overdo it either.

493
00:40:26,987 --> 00:40:35,080
[SPEAKER_01]: What about concentrating on a neutral object?

494
00:40:36,122 --> 00:40:39,808
[SPEAKER_01]: Would that take away all the unwholesome objects?

495
00:40:40,719 --> 00:40:44,141
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, no, because concentration for people today means absorption.

496
00:40:44,521 --> 00:40:47,183
[SPEAKER_03]: And if you're absorbed into something, means you're not aware of yourself.

497
00:40:47,763 --> 00:40:57,309
[SPEAKER_03]: So, how can you develop self-reflection and self-clarity, if the basic premise of yourself being present and seeing that in the situation is not there?

498
00:40:57,369 --> 00:41:01,272
[SPEAKER_03]: That's why most of the contemporary meditation techniques are completely misleading.

499
00:41:02,538 --> 00:41:05,980
[SPEAKER_03]: No doubt that the novelty experiences and peace come out of it.

500
00:41:06,660 --> 00:41:10,222
[SPEAKER_03]: But that peace is also peace at the cost of self-reflection.

501
00:41:10,802 --> 00:41:13,823
[SPEAKER_03]: Which means it's a peace that's peaceful only because it's agreeable.

502
00:41:14,444 --> 00:41:17,105
[SPEAKER_03]: Not because it's a genuine clarity or contains wisdom.

503
00:41:17,865 --> 00:41:19,686
[SPEAKER_03]: It's only because I find it agreeable.

504
00:41:21,667 --> 00:41:24,769
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's why often it ceases to be enough as well.

505
00:41:25,149 --> 00:41:31,812
[SPEAKER_03]: So then people need to do it harder, commit harder, to get more of that novelty back that's going to be a peaceful feeling.

506
00:41:33,653 --> 00:41:35,694
[SPEAKER_03]: But that's not the peace you want.

507
00:41:35,714 --> 00:41:37,754
[SPEAKER_03]: There's basically peace within the central domain.

508
00:41:38,835 --> 00:41:40,395
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's not peaceful.

509
00:41:40,435 --> 00:41:47,297
[SPEAKER_01]: What about the power of concentration?

510
00:41:48,478 --> 00:41:49,838
[SPEAKER_03]: You don't want power of concentration.

511
00:41:49,858 --> 00:41:51,178
[SPEAKER_03]: You want power of reflection.

512
00:41:52,179 --> 00:41:57,620
[SPEAKER_03]: And then, the right concentration will come out of that.

513
00:41:58,521 --> 00:41:59,761
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you don't, again,

514
00:42:01,283 --> 00:42:11,089
[SPEAKER_03]: When a person is committing to some practice of concentration on account of their muddled and foggy and obtuse thinking, no clarity of their thinking can arise.

515
00:42:12,410 --> 00:42:12,570
[SPEAKER_03]: Why?

516
00:42:12,610 --> 00:42:20,695
[SPEAKER_03]: Because that's basically... the tools they're using to develop their concentration are the ones affected by ignorance.

517
00:42:20,715 --> 00:42:24,878
[SPEAKER_03]: And if you're not seeing that, whatever you gain through them is just going to share the same nature.

518
00:42:29,337 --> 00:42:40,525
[SPEAKER_03]: Because people think that, well, as long as I practice concentration of any kind, that will transfer itself and I'll benefit from it in other, like, spiritual development.

519
00:42:41,866 --> 00:42:42,206
[SPEAKER_03]: You won't.

520
00:42:42,627 --> 00:42:45,669
[SPEAKER_03]: That development that you think is spiritual, it's really, like, sensual.

521
00:42:46,069 --> 00:42:50,372
[SPEAKER_03]: Which is why, yeah, most people's practice of spirituality is very much sensual.

522
00:42:51,573 --> 00:42:52,854
[SPEAKER_03]: It's a practice of sensuality.

523
00:42:57,200 --> 00:43:00,802
[SPEAKER_01]: Attaining physical experiences.

524
00:43:00,822 --> 00:43:01,642
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, yeah, exactly.

525
00:43:02,122 --> 00:43:12,987
[SPEAKER_03]: Basically attaining agreeable experiences without trying to see your motivation behind it or like fully understanding the nature of those experiences.

526
00:43:13,967 --> 00:43:19,029
[SPEAKER_03]: And you could say that any spiritual practice today, it's basically just spiritualizing sensuality.

527
00:43:20,995 --> 00:43:35,437
[SPEAKER_03]: The true spiritual practice comes in you undoing the sensuality, and then seeing what's there already, without you needing to have a desire for it, or inspiration, or whatever, some sort of spiritual uplifting.

528
00:43:37,600 --> 00:43:46,722
[SPEAKER_03]: That's why, that's why, if the dhamma is done rightly, the Buddha's teaching is done rightly, like, nobody can stumble upon it by accident or through some miraculous experience.

529
00:43:47,683 --> 00:43:48,783
[SPEAKER_03]: It's done through development.

530
00:43:48,843 --> 00:43:51,464
[SPEAKER_03]: It's achieved through development and basically hard work.

531
00:43:51,604 --> 00:43:58,506
[SPEAKER_03]: That's why the Buddha would just praise the diligence, self-reflection, not tolerating unwholesome states that you know are present in you.

532
00:43:58,546 --> 00:43:59,526
[SPEAKER_03]: And you're tolerating them.

533
00:43:59,966 --> 00:44:03,287
[SPEAKER_03]: By tolerating them, you give rise to motivation that's rooted in them.

534
00:44:05,447 --> 00:44:07,549
[SPEAKER_03]: By giving rise to that, sooner or later you're going to act out of it.

535
00:44:07,669 --> 00:44:12,693
[SPEAKER_03]: So you end up being fully responsible for the perpetuation of your own ignorance and lack of freedom.

536
00:44:33,915 --> 00:44:34,415
[SPEAKER_02]: What's going on?

537
00:44:35,596 --> 00:44:36,737
[SPEAKER_02]: Huh?

538
00:44:36,897 --> 00:44:42,702
[SPEAKER_02]: What's going on?

539
00:44:42,862 --> 00:44:45,385
[SPEAKER_03]: There's not enough room.

540
00:44:45,405 --> 00:44:46,565
[SPEAKER_00]: Is Jābū visible?

541
00:44:46,585 --> 00:44:46,866
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

542
00:44:47,146 --> 00:44:50,809
[SPEAKER_00]: Not really, eh?

543
